course: dictionary
lesson: 7472
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and context tells you which one is meant. Port is a good example. «Real ships could not do that: they often waited months in port for the wind to turn.» A ship cannot wait inside a computer, so port here means a town or harbour where ships stop.
explain: Port can also mean the left side of a ship when you face the front, or a hole on a computer where you plug in a cable. Look at the words around it to choose the right meaning. Ask: which meaning fits this sentence?
explain: Try the check: swap in your meaning and see if the sentence still makes sense. «Venice held a chain of ports and islands along the Adriatic» only makes sense if ports are places where ships stop, so that is the meaning here.
explain: Phrases also use words in special ways. «Any port in a storm» means any help is welcome when you are in trouble. You will not find it by looking at each word alone, so read the whole phrase.
fact: A port is a town or place where ships stop to load and unload goods and people.
fact: «Port» can also mean the left side of a ship when you face the front.
fact: A port on a computer is a hole where you plug in a cable.
fact: Port is also a sweet, strong wine.
fact: «Any port in a storm» means any help is welcome when you are in trouble.
exercise: «The ship waited in port for the wind to change.» What does port mean here? | A sweet, strong drink for the crew | The right side of the ship | A hole for plugging in a cable | *A town where ships stop
exercise: The captain shouts, «Turn to port!» Which side of the ship is port? | The top deck | The back of the ship | The right side | *The left side when you face the front
exercise: Lara needs to plug her cable into the port at the back of her laptop. What is a port here? | A town on the sea | The left side of a boat | A sweet, strong wine | *A hole where you plug in a cable
test: «Bordeaux is a port on a river in south-west France.» What is a port here? | The left side of a ship | *A town where ships stop to load and unload | A sweet, strong wine | A hole on a computer
test: Mum says, «Any port in a storm», meaning when you are in trouble. What does it mean? | Only sail in calm weather | *Any help is welcome when you are in trouble | Ships must always sail to port | Always buy wine at the harbour
exam: Which sentence uses port to mean a sweet, strong wine? | The ship sailed into port at dawn | *Grandad sipped a glass of port after dinner | Captain, steer to port! | Plug the cable into the port at the back
